Stubs for 95-nexgen-storage-sanitize only.

Kept apart from ../stubs and ../stubs-hw because this script needs an nvme that
models SANICAP and the sanitize log, an lsblk that answers -J with a TRAN
column, and a findmnt that can claim the root filesystem is on a drive about to
be erased. run-all.sh prepends this directory ahead of stubs-hw for the
sanitisation tests alone, so nothing here perturbs the other scripts.

The machine modelled is the real 183 node: one NVMe namespace behind
/dev/nvme0, and a MegaRAID volume at /dev/sda whose two SATA members lsblk
cannot see.

Destructive commands are never executed. Every one that WOULD have run is
appended to $STUB_STATE_DIR/stub-destructive-calls instead, so a test can assert
that audit mode issued none. Several stubs also read its presence as "this run
has erased something" when reporting residual filesystem signatures.

STUB_STATE_DIR defaults to /tmp but run-all.sh points it at a directory of its
own, so two suite runs -- or a suite and an ad-hoc run alongside it -- cannot
clobber each other's record of what was issued. Sharing fixed /tmp paths made
the suite fail differently on each run, which reads as flaky code and is not.

nvme      SANICAP and FNA per STUB_SANICAP/STUB_FNA; the sanitize log advances
          through STUB_SANITIZE_TICKS in-progress polls to a terminal SSTAT set
          by STUB_SANITIZE_RESULT. STUB_SANITIZE_JSON=0 removes -o json support
          so the text parser is exercised. STUB_SANITIZE_LIES=1 makes the text
          log claim success while SSTAT reports failure -- the vendor-wording
          trap the runbook itself warns about.
lsblk     one namespace per controller plus the MegaRAID volume;
          STUB_DIRECT_SATA=1 adds a directly attached SSD, STUB_MOUNTED_TARGET
          gives a device a mountpoint, STUB_NS_MISMATCH=1 hides a namespace
          that nvme list declares, STUB_NO_MOUNTPOINTS=1 models pre-2.37
          util-linux which has no MOUNTPOINTS column. STUB_STALE_FSTYPE keeps
          answering FSTYPE after an erase, as udev's record of the old
          partitions does on a real host.
blkid     -p probe of the media: empty once anything is erased.
          STUB_RESIDUAL_PTTYPE returns a partition table that survived.
blockdev  --getsize64 and --rereadpt only.
findmnt   overlay root by default; STUB_ROOT_FSTYPE=ext4 models the case
          section 6 forbids outright.
hdparm    -I reports the ATA SANITIZE feature set per STUB_ATA_CAPS;
          --sanitize-status advances like the nvme log, per STUB_ATA_RESULT.
smartctl  megaraid members answer with exit 4 and a good identity, as the real
          controller does. STUB_NO_SERIAL=1 blanks a serial, which is a section
          7 stop condition.
sg_inq    serial per /dev/sg node; STUB_SG_PASSTHROUGH=1 exposes the RAID
          members, which is the one purge path that needs no vendor binary.
sg_opcodes / sg_sanitize   SCSI SANITIZE support and execution.
hostname  a fixed name, so a generated fixture does not carry the hostname of
          whichever workstation produced it.
id        reports uid 0. The script refuses to run unprivileged and the suite
          is not root.
apt-get   exits 0. Package installation is not what these tests exercise.
